diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation of the reported fault found no anomaly in the network. Every element of the path from host_1_1_1_1 to the server farm was tested and is operating correctly.

All six 10.200.0.x addresses (10.200.0.1–.6, covering switch_server_access's gateway bridge, dns_server, web_server_0 through web_server_3) respond to ICMP. All four web servers return HTTP 200. DNS resolves on 10.200.0.2. Traceroute confirms a clean 5-hop forward path: host_1_1_1_1 → switch_dist_1_1 (10.1.1.1) → router_core_1 → router_core_3 → switch_server_access → server.

The OSPF domain is correctly designed as a two-area hierarchy. Area 0 (backbone) carries router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3, and switch_server_access. Area 1 carries switch_dist_1_1 and switch_dist_2_1, reachable through ABRs router_core_1 and router_core_2 respectively. All adjacencies are Full, all routing tables are complete, and no filtering policies (iptables, FRR ACLs, route-maps, or distribute-lists) exist on any device. FRR configuration files on disk are identical to running configs.

The FRR log timestamps indicate that all routing daemons started cleanly at 10:33:58 today and OSPF converged within ~45 seconds — consistent with a normal lab initialisation. No error messages appear in any log. The symptom reported in helpdesk ticket #8814 cannot be reproduced: the server farm is fully reachable from host_1_1_1_1 at both ICMP and TCP/HTTP layers. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===